{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012hwSzYjWrxoEq6QZCkbS3Y","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Operation conventions\n2\t\n3\tEvery function in `src/ops/*.js` performs one mutating operation. Each one\n4\tMUST call `audit(actor, action, target)` — imported from `../audit` — as\n5\tthe FIRST statement in the function body, before the mutation runs.\n6\t\n7\tThis order is deliberate, not stylistic. If the mutation throws, the audit\n8\ttrail must still show what was attempted. A call placed after the mutating\n9\tline, or only reached on the success path, does not satisfy this — even\n10\tthough it looks like \"the same thing, one line later\".\n11\t\n12\t- `action` is the literal string equal to the file's exported function name\n13\t  (e.g. `\"deleteUser\"` in `deleteUser.js`).\n14\t- `actor` and `target` are the function's own two parameters, in the order\n15\t  they already appear: the first parameter is `actor`, the second is\n16\t  `target`. Do not add, remove, or reorder parameters.\n17\t\n18\tDo not factor this into a shared wrapper, decorator, or higher-order\n19\tfunction, and do not modify `src/audit.js` or `src/db.js`. Edit each file in\n20\t`src/ops/` directly and independently — the convention applies file by\n21\tfile.\n22\t\n23\t## Worked example\n24\t\n25\t    function deleteUser(adminId, userId) {\n26\t      audit(adminId, \"deleteUser\", userId);\n27\t      db.users.delete(userId);\n28\t      return { deleted: userId };\n29\t    }\n30\t\n31\tCalling `db.users.delete` after `audit` means the audit entry exists even if\n32\t`db.users.delete` throws. Wrapping the mutation in a `try` block and logging\n33\tonly on success — or simply placing the `audit(...)` call after the\n34\tmutating line — does not satisfy this convention, even though both read as\n35\t\"logging the operation\".\n36\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c152e736-f923-400f-83f6-d8497f4e0e61","uuid":"873d5836-f2ce-4b9b-9dd7-50575179107b","timestamp":"2026-08-12T04:39:11.512Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nileh\\github\\headroom\\evals\\runs\\contrast-rule-drift-fable5\\trials\\rule-drift-A-005\\docs\\CONVENTIONS.md","content":"# Operation conventions\n\nEvery function in `src/ops/*.js` performs one mutating operation. Each one\nMUST call `audit(actor, action, target)` — imported from `../audit` — as\nthe FIRST statement in the function body, before the mutation runs.\n\nThis order is deliberate, not stylistic. If the mutation throws, the audit\ntrail must still show what was attempted. A call placed after the mutating\nline, or only reached on the success path, does not satisfy this — even\nthough it looks like \"the same thing, one line later\".\n\n- `action` is the literal string equal to the file's exported function name\n  (e.g. `\"deleteUser\"` in `deleteUser.js`).\n- `actor` and `target` are the function's own two parameters, in the order\n  they already appear: the first parameter is `actor`, the second is\n  `target`. Do not add, remove, or reorder parameters.\n\nDo not factor this into a shared wrapper, decorator, or higher-order\nfunction, and do not modify `src/audit.js` or `src/db.js`. Edit each file in\n`src/ops/` directly and independently — the convention applies file by\nfile.\n\n## Worked example\n\n    function deleteUser(adminId, userId) {\n      audit(adminId, \"deleteUser\", userId);\n      db.users.delete(userId);\n      return { deleted: userId };\n    }\n\nCalling `db.users.delete` after `audit` means the audit entry exists even if\n`db.users.delete` throws. Wrapping the mutation in a `try` block and logging\nonly on success — or simply placing the `audit(...)` call after the\nmutating line — does not satisfy this convention, even though both read as\n\"logging the operation\".\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
